<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kimigayo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words c. 920; melody <a href="/years/1880">1880</a>; national anthem <a href="/years/1888">1888</a>; recognised in law <a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as 君が代</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The words of &quot;Kimigayo&quot; are an anonymous waka poem first found in the Kokin Wakashū, an anthology of about 920, which makes them the oldest words of any national anthem. The poem was copied into many later anthologies and sung by people of every rank as a wish for long life, at birthdays and other informal occasions, so no music was written down for it until the 19th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the annexation of Korea by Japan in 1910, Japan's &quot;<b>Kimigayo</b>&quot; became the official anthem and the Korean one was banned.</blockquote><a href="/songs/aegukga">Aegukga</a></li><li><blockquote>The words of &quot;<b>Kimigayo</b>&quot; are an anonymous waka poem first found in the Kokin Wakashū, an anthology of about 920, which makes them the oldest words of any national anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a></li></ul></section>
